INSERT INTO public.qb_questions (subject_id, difficulty, question_type, question_text, options, correct_answer, explanation, points, tags) VALUES
('e512054c-240f-461d-9ccd-e6cc5c9e6411', 'advanced', 'multiple_choice', 'A texturizing engineer explains that increasing DRAW RATIO during draw-texturing (POY→DTY) improves tenacity but REDUCES achievable crimp/bulk elasticity beyond a certain point. Why do these two properties (strength and crimp elasticity) trade off against each other as draw ratio increases?', '["Draw ratio has no relationship to either tenacity or crimp elasticity, making this described tradeoff technically nonexistent","Higher draw ratio increases molecular orientation/crystallinity along the filament axis (directly improving tenacity, as established earlier), but this SAME increased molecular order/rigidity also reduces the polymer''s remaining capacity for the FURTHER localized deformation needed to form and retain a pronounced crimp structure during the subsequent false-twist texturing step — the filament becomes progressively ''less able'' to take on new crimp deformation as it becomes more highly oriented/crystalline from drawing","Increasing draw ratio always improves BOTH tenacity and crimp elasticity simultaneously with no tradeoff whatsoever, contradicting the premise of this described relationship","Crimp elasticity is entirely determined by the texturing temperature alone, with zero relationship to the draw ratio applied during the same process"]'::jsonb, 'Higher draw ratio increases molecular orientation/crystallinity along the filament axis (directly improving tenacity, as established earlier), but this SAME increased molecular order/rigidity also reduces the polymer''s remaining capacity for the FURTHER localized deformation needed to form and retain a pronounced crimp structure during the subsequent false-twist texturing step — the filament becomes progressively ''less able'' to take on new crimp deformation as it becomes more highly oriented/crystalline from drawing', 'This connects the earlier draw-ratio/molecular-orientation and false-twist-texturing crimp mechanisms into a genuine engineering tradeoff — the SAME molecular change that improves tenacity (increased chain orientation/crystallinity from drawing) also makes the polymer progressively more rigid/resistant to the FURTHER localized bending/deformation needed to form and hold a pronounced crimp during subsequent texturing, meaning a texturing engineer must balance draw ratio against BOTH target properties simultaneously rather than simply maximizing draw ratio for strength alone, since doing so would come at a genuine cost to the yarn''s bulk/crimp/stretch character.', 3, '{"draw-ratio-tenacity-crimp-tradeoff"}'::text[]),
('e512054c-240f-461d-9ccd-e6cc5c9e6411', 'advanced', 'multiple_choice', 'A yarn technologist explains that ''dtex'' (decitex, grams per 10,000 meters) is increasingly used INSTEAD of denier (grams per 9,000 meters) in modern international technical specifications, even though both are direct (linear-density) systems measuring essentially the same physical property. What is the core practical reason for this shift?', '["Dtex and denier measure fundamentally different physical properties, making this comparison technically invalid from the start","Dtex is based on the metric system (10,000 meters, a round decimal-friendly reference length) consistent with SI/metric units used throughout the rest of international technical/scientific specification, while denier''s 9,000-meter reference length is a historical, non-decimal-friendly holdover — the shift toward dtex reflects broader international standardization toward SI-consistent units, avoiding the need for an awkward, non-round conversion factor when working alongside other metric-based textile and engineering specifications","Dtex measures yarn count using an indirect (length-per-weight) system, the exact opposite of denier''s direct (weight-per-length) system, making them fundamentally incompatible measurement philosophies","There is no meaningful difference between dtex and denier of any kind, making the entire premise of this question about a deliberate international shift factually incorrect"]'::jsonb, 'Dtex is based on the metric system (10,000 meters, a round decimal-friendly reference length) consistent with SI/metric units used throughout the rest of international technical/scientific specification, while denier''s 9,000-meter reference length is a historical, non-decimal-friendly holdover — the shift toward dtex reflects broader international standardization toward SI-consistent units, avoiding the need for an awkward, non-round conversion factor when working alongside other metric-based textile and engineering specifications', 'Both dtex and denier are DIRECT (weight-per-length) count systems measuring the identical underlying physical property (linear density), differing only in their reference length (10,000m vs 9,000m) — dtex''s round, decimal-friendly 10,000m basis aligns cleanly with the broader SI/metric system used throughout international technical and scientific specification, while denier''s historical 9,000m basis is an awkward non-round holdover requiring a less convenient conversion factor (denier = dtex × 0.9) — this practical standardization convenience, not any fundamental difference in what''s being measured, is why dtex has increasingly become the preferred international technical specification unit, particularly in technical/industrial yarn contexts working alongside other metric engineering specifications.', 3, '{"dtex-vs-denier-standardization"}'::text[]),
('e512054c-240f-461d-9ccd-e6cc5c9e6411', 'advanced', 'multiple_choice', 'A synthetic filament producer''s R&D team develops a ''self-texturing'' bicomponent filament (side-by-side two-polymer arrangement with different shrinkage rates) intended to crimp automatically during a simple heat-relaxation step, eliminating the need for a separate mechanical false-twist texturing machine entirely. What is the KEY economic/process tradeoff of this self-texturing approach versus conventional false-twist texturing?', '["Self-texturing bicomponent filament offers no tradeoff whatsoever and is unconditionally superior to conventional texturing in every respect with zero downside","Self-texturing eliminates a separate, capital-intensive texturing machine/process step (a genuine simplification and cost savings), but requires MORE SOPHISTICATED, precisely-controlled EXTRUSION technology (accurately co-extruding two different polymer streams side-by-side within a single filament) — trading texturing-STAGE process complexity/cost for EXTRUSION-STAGE complexity/cost, rather than eliminating complexity altogether","Self-texturing bicomponent filament can only be produced from a single polymer type, contradicting the fundamental premise of a bicomponent (two-polymer) filament structure","Conventional false-twist texturing is always technically impossible for any polyester-type polymer, making this entire technology comparison factually inapplicable"]'::jsonb, 'Self-texturing eliminates a separate, capital-intensive texturing machine/process step (a genuine simplification and cost savings), but requires MORE SOPHISTICATED, precisely-controlled EXTRUSION technology (accurately co-extruding two different polymer streams side-by-side within a single filament) — trading texturing-STAGE process complexity/cost for EXTRUSION-STAGE complexity/cost, rather than eliminating complexity altogether', 'This extends the earlier bicomponent-fiber discussion with a genuine process-economics insight — self-texturing bicomponent technology doesn''t eliminate complexity, it RELOCATES it: instead of needing a separate downstream false-twist texturing machine/process (with its own capital cost and process step), the complexity shifts UPSTREAM into the extrusion stage itself, which now requires much more precise co-extrusion control (accurately maintaining a consistent side-by-side two-polymer cross-section throughout spinning) — evaluating whether this technology is genuinely advantageous requires comparing the TOTAL cost/complexity tradeoff across the full process, not assuming eliminating one visible process step automatically means overall simplification.', 3, '{"self-texturing-bicomponent-tradeoff"}'::text[]),
('e512054c-240f-461d-9ccd-e6cc5c9e6411', 'advanced', 'multiple_choice', 'A yarn testing lab reports a filament yarn''s ''shrinkage force'' (the actual physical force the yarn exerts as it attempts to shrink when heated under restraint, measured in a thermal-mechanical analyzer) as a SEPARATE parameter from simple ''shrinkage percentage'' (how much it shrinks when heated freely, unrestrained). Why does shrinkage FORCE matter as an additional, distinct property for certain applications, beyond just shrinkage percentage?', '["Shrinkage force and shrinkage percentage always measure mathematically identical information, making tracking both entirely redundant with zero additional insight from either","In applications where the yarn/fabric is heat-treated WHILE under some mechanical restraint (e.g., during heat-setting within a garment or composite structure where the material cannot freely shrink), the relevant property isn''t how much it WOULD shrink if free, but how much FORCE it exerts while being prevented from shrinking — this force can cause fabric/seam distortion, puckering, or even structural damage in a constrained assembly, a distinct failure mode that shrinkage percentage (measuring free, unconstrained shrinkage) doesn''t directly predict","Shrinkage force is only relevant for testing metal wire, never for any textile filament yarn, making this property irrelevant to yarn technology","A yarn with HIGH shrinkage percentage always automatically has LOW shrinkage force, and vice versa, making the two properties perfectly inversely predictable from one another"]'::jsonb, 'In applications where the yarn/fabric is heat-treated WHILE under some mechanical restraint (e.g., during heat-setting within a garment or composite structure where the material cannot freely shrink), the relevant property isn''t how much it WOULD shrink if free, but how much FORCE it exerts while being prevented from shrinking — this force can cause fabric/seam distortion, puckering, or even structural damage in a constrained assembly, a distinct failure mode that shrinkage percentage (measuring free, unconstrained shrinkage) doesn''t directly predict', 'This is a genuinely advanced materials-testing insight — shrinkage PERCENTAGE and shrinkage FORCE measure two DIFFERENT physical scenarios: percentage describes what happens when a sample is heated completely FREE to shrink, while force describes what happens when a sample is heated but mechanically PREVENTED from shrinking (a scenario common in real assembled products — a yarn constrained within a woven/knitted structure, or fabric heat-set within a bonded/laminated construction) — in these constrained real-world scenarios, the yarn''s shrinkage FORCE (not its free shrinkage percentage) is what actually determines whether it will distort, pucker, or structurally stress the surrounding constrained assembly, making it a genuinely distinct, separately-relevant property for certain technical applications.', 3, '{"shrinkage-force-vs-percentage"}'::text[]),
('e512054c-240f-461d-9ccd-e6cc5c9e6411', 'advanced', 'multiple_choice', 'A technical yarn producer explains that ''creep'' (gradual, permanent elongation under sustained LOW-level tension over a LONG time period, distinct from immediate elastic stretch or ultimate breaking elongation) is a critical property for yarns used in LONG-TERM load-bearing applications (like suspension cables, tie-downs, or structural reinforcement), even when that tension is well below the yarn''s breaking strength. Why does a yarn''s short-term tenacity/elongation-at-break testing NOT predict its creep behavior?', '["Short-term tenacity testing and long-term creep behavior always measure mathematically identical underlying properties, making creep testing an unnecessary redundant test","Creep is a TIME-DEPENDENT, VISCOELASTIC phenomenon — polymer chains can gradually slip/rearrange under SUSTAINED (even quite low) tension over long time periods through a fundamentally different physical mechanism than the near-instantaneous mechanical failure measured in a standard short-duration tenacity/elongation test, meaning a yarn can show excellent short-term strength/elongation properties while still exhibiting problematic long-term creep under sustained real-world load","A yarn with excellent short-term tenacity always automatically has zero creep behavior under any sustained load condition, making separate creep testing unnecessary whenever short-term strength is already verified","Creep only affects natural fibers like cotton, never synthetic technical yarns, making this concern about synthetic technical yarn creep testing technically inapplicable"]'::jsonb, 'Creep is a TIME-DEPENDENT, VISCOELASTIC phenomenon — polymer chains can gradually slip/rearrange under SUSTAINED (even quite low) tension over long time periods through a fundamentally different physical mechanism than the near-instantaneous mechanical failure measured in a standard short-duration tenacity/elongation test, meaning a yarn can show excellent short-term strength/elongation properties while still exhibiting problematic long-term creep under sustained real-world load', 'This is an advanced materials-science distinction relevant to technical/industrial yarn applications — standard tenacity/elongation-at-break testing measures near-INSTANTANEOUS mechanical failure behavior (how much force/stretch until immediate breakage), while CREEP is a fundamentally different, TIME-DEPENDENT viscoelastic phenomenon (gradual polymer chain slippage/rearrangement under SUSTAINED, even low-level, tension over long time periods) — these are genuinely different physical mechanisms operating on different timescales, meaning a yarn''s excellent short-term strength test results provide NO guarantee about its long-term creep behavior under sustained real-world load, which is exactly why technical yarns for long-term load-bearing applications (suspension, tie-downs, structural reinforcement) require SEPARATE, dedicated long-duration creep testing rather than relying on standard short-term strength specifications alone.', 3, '{"creep-behavior-technical-yarn"}'::text[]);
